  <!-- ===================== SECTION 6: INSIGHTS ===================== -->
  <div class="section" id="s6">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-num">6</div>
      <div>
        <div class="section-title">Key Insights &amp; Recommendations</div>
        <div class="section-subtitle">Data-driven recommendations for product feature development and user engagement strategies</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">

      <div class="insight-grid">

        <div class="insight-card">
          <div class="ic-num">1</div>
          <h4>December &amp; March: Natural Activation Windows</h4>
          <p>December (11,113 avg steps) and March (10,976) are the two highest-activity months — each ~8–9% above the June trough (10,267). These are not random noise; they represent consistent seasonal peaks across all 11 years.</p>
          <div class="rec">&#127919; Launch new challenge features, streak campaigns, and push notification series in late November and mid-February to ride natural engagement upswings.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="insight-card">
          <div class="ic-num">2</div>
          <h4>August Stress Spike Demands a Wellness Response</h4>
          <p>August is the peak stress month (avg 5.79/10), 13% above September's low (5.11). Meanwhile, September records the lowest stress in any month. This makes August a critical window for wellness intervention.</p>
          <div class="rec">&#127919; Deploy stress-management nudges, breathing exercise prompts, and sleep coaching campaigns in August. Use September's low-stress window to re-engage users with new goal-setting features.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="insight-card">
          <div class="ic-num">3</div>
          <h4>Steps Are the Single Best Engagement Proxy</h4>
          <p>Step count has a near-perfect correlation with calories burned (r = 0.97). No other metric — sleep, HR, water, stress — shows more than negligible correlation with daily steps. This makes steps uniquely reliable as an activity engagement signal.</p>
          <div class="rec">&#127919; Focus engagement dashboards and team challenges on step counts as the primary KPI. Calories, HR, and sleep are independent wellness dimensions that should be tracked and nudged separately.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="insight-card">
          <div class="ic-num">4</div>
          <h4>Running = Highest Activity, Lowest Stress</h4>
          <p>Running users average the highest step count (10,721) and the lowest stress level (5.36) among all workout types. Yoga users have the lowest share of "Tired" mood days (31.3% vs. 35.1% for Running).</p>
          <div class="rec">&#127919; Promote Running for users showing high-step / high-calorie goals; promote Yoga for users showing elevated stress or high tired-mood frequency. Personalized workout recommendations by goal/mood state can drive meaningful differentiation.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="insight-card">
          <div class="ic-num">5</div>
          <h4>Tired Days Correlate with High Activity, Not Low</h4>
